target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestLoadsStructuralRulesThatNothingElseAsserts'
expect: a key Go folds onto a field and the Python never reads is unreadable
why:    the fifth distinct mechanism for the same forgery, and the one four review cycles missed — found by CodeRabbit on the PR. encoding/json matches struct tags CASE-INSENSITIVELY and Go has no case-sensitive mode, so `{"Applied":0}` decodes into Cycle.Applied while the Python's exact `rec.get("applied")` reads nothing. Measured on a store whose earlier cycle applied 5: python=halted, go=CONVERGED. Same for a duplicate differing only in case (`applied` beside `APPLIED`, last key wins in Go), and for the envelope's own `Run_ID` and `PHASE`. misplacedRunField cannot see any of it, because these keys fold onto fields the row's phase legitimately owns.
